# ======================================================================
# actuarial-severity-curves.agg  --  severity distributions used in insurance
# ======================================================================
#
# Version: 1
#
# PURPOSE
# -------
# scipy.stats ships 100+ continuous distributions; actuaries use a small,
# well-trodden subset. This file is a REFERENCE SET: one minimal
# aggregate per distribution that has real support in the loss-modelling
# literature, showing how to write it in DecL. Every program is the
# simplest possible carrier --
#
#     agg <Name> 1 claim sev <severity> fixed
#
# -- a fixed single claim, so the aggregate IS the severity and you can
# read its mean / cv / tail straight off `qd(build(...))`. Swap in a real
# frequency (poisson, mixed gamma, ...) and limits to use one for real.
#
# This is NOT loaded by default. Load it explicitly:
#     from aggregate import Underwriter
#     uw = Underwriter(databases='actuarial-severity-curves')
# or add "actuarial-severity-curves" to [build] databases in config.toml.
#
# SELECTION -- what counts as "relevant", and why
# -----------------------------------------------
# The picks (and their rough frequency in the sources below) are:
#   lognormal, gamma, exponential, Weibull, inverse Gaussian -- the
#     standard light/moderate-tailed severities (Klugman et al., Parodi);
#   the GB2 family -- generalized gamma, beta-prime (B2), Burr XII
#     (Singh-Maddala), Burr III (Dagum), log-logistic (Fisk) -- the
#     flexible four/three-parameter curves that nest most of the others;
#   Pareto (Type I), Lomax (Type II / shifted Pareto), the Generalized
#     Pareto (peaks-over-threshold), inverse gamma, Frechet -- the
#     heavy / power-law tails for large losses and reinsurance;
#   Gumbel and the GEV -- extreme-value laws for block maxima;
#   beta -- bounded severity, and the gateway to exposure-curve models.
#
# References (mentioned per the file's brief; cite by author/year):
#   * Klugman, Panjer & Willmot, *Loss Models* -- Appendix A is the
#     canonical actuarial severity table (the parameterizations below
#     follow its families).
#   * Parodi (2015), *Pricing in General Insurance*; Parodi, Thrumble &
#     Watson (2024), *Loss Modelling from First Principles*; Parodi &
#     Watson (2019), property-graph model of fire/explosion losses --
#     lognormal, gamma, Weibull, Pareto/GPD as the working severities.
#   * McDonald & Richards (1987), hazard rates of the generalized beta
#     distributions; Cummins, Dionne, McDonald & Pritchett (1990),
#     applications of the GB2 family to insurance loss processes; Venter
#     (1983), transformed beta/gamma -- the GB2 umbrella: it nests the
#     log-t, generalized gamma, B2, Burr III & XII, Lomax, Fisk,
#     lognormal (limit), gamma and Weibull.
#   * Bernegger (1997), *The Swiss Re Exposure Curves and the MBBEFD
#     Distribution Class*, ASTIN Bulletin -- the two-parameter MBBEFD
#     family for exposure rating (see the note in section E).
#
# DecL severity reminder: `scale * scipy_name shape... + loc` follows
# scipy's (shape, loc, scale) convention; for a few families aggregate
# also accepts the actuarial `name mean cv` form (used below where
# natural). All shapes are scipy's; means here are ~10k for comparison.

# Contents
# --------
# A. Light and moderate tails
# B. Lognormal and the GB2 family
# C. Heavy and power-law tails
# D. Extreme-value distributions
# E. Bounded severity and exposure curves


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# A. Light and moderate tails
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The exponential is memoryless (constant hazard) -- the reference point
# everything else is "more" or "less" tailed than. scale = mean, cv = 1.
agg A.Exponential 1 claim sev 10000 * expon fixed note{Exponential: constant hazard, cv = 1. scipy expon; scale = mean.};
# Gamma: shape > 1 gives an interior mode; lighter-than-exponential tail.
# Shown in the actuary-friendly mean/cv form.
agg A.Gamma 1 claim sev gamma 10000 cv 0.8 fixed note{Gamma severity by mean and cv (shape = 1/cv^2). Klugman et al.; Parodi.};
# Weibull: hazard rises (c>1) or falls (c<1) with size; a workhorse for
# attritional losses (Parodi). scipy weibull_min, shape c.
agg A.Weibull 1 claim sev 10000 * weibull_min 1.5 fixed note{Weibull (weibull_min), shape c = 1.5: increasing hazard.};
# Inverse Gaussian: right-skewed, heavier than gamma; common in pure-
# premium / Tweedie work. mean/cv form supported.
agg A.InverseGaussian 1 claim sev invgauss 10000 cv 0.7 fixed note{Inverse Gaussian by mean and cv -- right-skewed, Tweedie-adjacent.}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# B. Lognormal and the GB2 family
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lognormal is the most-used severity in the literature (Parodi); a tail
# between the light families and the power laws. mean/cv form.
agg B.Lognormal 1 claim sev lognorm 10000 cv 1.5 fixed note{Lognormal by mean and cv -- the default working severity.};
# The generalized (transformed) gamma -- Stacy's three-parameter law,
# the GB2's gamma-side limit; nests gamma (a-shape), Weibull and
# lognormal. scipy gengamma shapes (a, c).
agg B.GenGamma 1 claim sev 10000 * gengamma 2 1.5 fixed note{Generalized (transformed) gamma, shapes a=2, c=1.5 -- nests gamma/Weibull/lognormal.};
# Beta-prime (beta of the second kind, B2): the GB2 with scale, the
# representative GB2 member in scipy. scipy betaprime shapes (a, b).
agg B.BetaPrime 1 claim sev 10000 * betaprime 3 4 fixed note{Beta-prime / beta-of-the-2nd-kind (B2) -- the GB2 representative. Cummins et al. (1990).};
# Burr XII (Singh-Maddala): GB2 with p=1; a flexible heavy-bodied curve
# heavily used for liability. scipy burr12 shapes (c, d).
agg B.Burr12 1 claim sev 10000 * burr12 3 1.2 fixed note{Burr XII / Singh-Maddala (GB2, p=1), shapes c=3, d=1.2.};
# Burr III (Dagum): the GB2 with q=1, the Burr XII's mirror. scipy burr.
agg B.BurrIII 1 claim sev 10000 * burr 3 1.2 fixed note{Burr III / Dagum (GB2, q=1), shapes c=3, d=1.2.};
# Log-logistic (Fisk): GB2 with p=q=1; logistic on the log scale, a
# moderately heavy tail. scipy fisk shape c.
agg B.LogLogistic 1 claim sev 10000 * fisk 2.5 fixed note{Log-logistic / Fisk (GB2, p=q=1), shape c=2.5.}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# C. Heavy and power-law tails
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Power-law tails: tail index drives finiteness of moments. Shape 2.5 is
# used so the variance is finite (the auto-sizer refuses infinite
# variance without an explicit grid -- see examples.agg I.Heavy).
#
# Pareto Type I (single-parameter / European), shifted to ground-up so
# support starts at 0. scipy pareto shape b; "- 10000" undoes the scale
# floor. Tail index alpha = b.
agg C.Pareto 1 claim sev 10000 * pareto 2.5 - 10000 fixed note{Pareto Type I (European), alpha = 2.5, shifted ground-up.};
# Lomax (Pareto Type II / American / shifted Pareto): support from 0
# natively, the GB2's Lomax member. scipy lomax shape c = alpha.
agg C.Lomax 1 claim sev 10000 * lomax 2.5 fixed note{Lomax / Pareto Type II (shifted Pareto), alpha = 2.5.};
# Generalized Pareto (GPD): the peaks-over-threshold tail of extreme-
# value theory; xi = shape c sets tail heaviness (xi<1 finite mean).
agg C.GPD 1 claim sev 10000 * genpareto 0.3 fixed note{Generalized Pareto (GPD), xi = 0.3 -- the peaks-over-threshold tail (EVT).};
# Inverse gamma: a heavy right tail (reciprocal of a gamma), a Klugman
# staple. scipy invgamma shape a; alpha = a sets tail.
agg C.InverseGamma 1 claim sev 10000 * invgamma 4.07 fixed note{Inverse gamma, shape a = 4.07 -- heavy right tail (Klugman et al.).}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# D. Extreme-value distributions
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Block-maxima laws (Fisher-Tippett): the limiting distribution of the
# largest loss in a period. Use for "largest event" / cat modelling, not
# usually for ground-up attritional severity.
#
# Gumbel: the light-tailed (type I) extreme-value law. scipy gumbel_r,
# location + scale (no shape).
agg D.Gumbel 1 claim sev 5000 * gumbel_r + 20000 fixed note{Gumbel (type I extreme value) -- block maxima, light tail.};
# Frechet: the heavy-tailed (type II) extreme-value law = inverse
# Weibull. scipy invweibull shape c (tail index).
agg D.Frechet 1 claim sev 10000 * invweibull 3 fixed note{Frechet / inverse Weibull (type II extreme value), shape c = 3.};
# Generalized extreme value (GEV): the unified EVT family; scipy
# genextreme shape c (c<0 is the heavy Frechet domain).
agg D.GEV 1 claim sev 10000 * genextreme -0.2 + 20000 fixed note{Generalized extreme value (GEV), shape -0.2 (Frechet domain).}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# E. Bounded severity and exposure curves
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Beta on [0, scale]: a bounded severity -- natural for a loss as a
# fraction of a known sum insured (a "destruction rate"). scipy beta
# shapes (a, b); here scaled to [0, 100000].
agg E.Beta 1 claim sev 100000 * beta 2 5 fixed note{Beta on [0, 100000] -- bounded severity / destruction rate, shapes a=2, b=5.}
# NOTE -- MBBEFD (Bernegger 1997). The Swiss Re exposure-curve standard
# for property per-risk rating is the two-parameter MBBEFD class
# (Maxwell-Boltzmann / Bose-Einstein / Fermi-Dirac) on the destruction
# ratio [0, 1]. It is an exposure-CURVE family, not a scipy.stats
# continuous rv, so it has no `sev mbbefd ...` form here; the bounded
# beta above is the closest in-vocabulary stand-in. Exposure-curve
# support is tracked separately.


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# F. Severity curves from cited papers (example-library review, batch 1)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Explicit published severity curves harvested from the batch-1 paper
# assessment (docs/example_library/batch-1.md). Unlike sections A-E these
# are reusable `sev` OBJECTS, named <CitationKey>.<Curve>, so a post can
# plug one into any frequency/limit, e.g. Bodoff's XPL mixture:
#     agg X [n1 n2] claims [inf 1000000] xs 0 sev.Bodoff2017.Pareto ...
# To inspect one on its own:  qd(build('agg t 1 claim sev.<Name> fixed')).
# note{} carries the citation key. Pareto naming follows the section C
# European = Type I (`pareto`) / American = Type II (`lomax`) split.
# Heavy members (alpha < 2 infinite variance, alpha < 1 infinite mean)
# are fine as definitions; when built they need a limit or explicit grid.

# Berens, R. M., "Reinsurance contracts with a multi-year aggregate limit" (1997)
# Lognormal severity, mean 150,000, sd 37,500 (cv = 0.25).
sev Berens1997.Lognorm lognorm 150000 cv 0.25 note{Berens1997};
# Single-parameter (European / Type I) Pareto, shape alpha = 1.5, threshold
# theta = 50,000 (support from 50,000; mean 150,000). Infinite variance.
# Paper also uses a 300,000-mean variant (theta = 100,000).
sev Berens1997.Pareto 50000 * pareto 1.5 note{Berens1997};

# Mack, T. & Fackler, M., "Exposure-rating in liability reinsurance" (2003)
# Lognormal baseline, sigma = 1.8 (cv ~ 4.953); mean nominal 10,000.
sev Mack2003.Lognorm lognorm 10000 cv 4.953 note{Mack2003};
# Riebesell tail Pareto, z = 20% => alpha = 0.737 (Lomax). alpha < 1 =>
# infinite mean; meaningful only inside a finite layer/limit.
sev Mack2003.RiebesellPareto 10000 * lomax 0.737 note{Mack2003};
# "American" (Type II / Lomax) Pareto, alpha = 1.5; infinite variance.
sev Mack2003.AmericanPareto 10000 * lomax 1.5 note{Mack2003};

# Mata, A. J. & Verheyen, M. A., "An Improved Method for Experience Rating
# Excess of Loss Treaties using Exposure Rating Techniques" (2005)
# Lognormal severity (2005 level), mu = 9.31, sigma = 2.29
# (scale = exp(9.31) ~ 11,048; mean ~ 152,000, cv ~ 13.7).
sev Mata2005.Lognorm 11048 * lognorm 2.29 note{Mata2005};

# Bodoff, N. M., "An Actuarial Model of Excess of Policy Limits Losses" (2017)
# Two-parameter (Type II / Lomax) Pareto, theta = 50,000, alpha = 1.5
# (mean 100,000). Infinite variance; the XPL model conditions the severity
# *before* the policy limit, then mixes over the limit profile.
sev Bodoff2017.Pareto 50000 * lomax 1.5 note{Bodoff2017};

# Jin, T., Provost, S. B. & Ren, J., "Moment-based density approximations
# for aggregate losses" (2016)
# Gamma test severity, shape 3, scale 2 (mean 6, cv = 1/sqrt(3)).
sev Jin2016.Gamma gamma 6 cv 0.5774 note{Jin2016};
# Two-parameter (Type II / Lomax) Pareto test severity, alpha = 20,
# theta = 100 (the lighter Pareto(5, 100) variant also appears in the paper).
sev Jin2016.Pareto 100 * lomax 20 note{Jin2016};

# Kang, J. S. J., Provost, S. B. & Ren, J., "Moment-Based Density Approximation
# Techniques as Applied to Heavy-tailed Distributions" (2019)
# Pareto Type II (Lomax), shape alpha = 3.5, scale beta = 3.5 (mean 1.4).
sev Kang2019.Pareto 3.5 * lomax 3.5 note{Kang2019};
